WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.224 --> 00:00:16.070
I joined my family, who were safely inside Rockburg.

2
00:00:16.931 --> 00:00:19.612
With pounding hearts, we watched the giant snake's approach.

3
00:00:19.632 --> 00:00:21.773
"It's a boa constrictor!"

4
00:00:22.194 --> 00:00:22.694
I whispered.

5
00:00:23.574 --> 00:00:26.536
The boys fired their guns, one right after another.

6
00:00:27.517 --> 00:00:28.757
I also fired and missed.

7
00:00:29.718 --> 00:00:33.240
Startled but unhurt, the snake glided into the nearby marsh.

8
00:00:34.292 --> 00:00:37.393
"No one is to leave the cave without my permission," I said sternly.

9
00:00:37.413 --> 00:00:39.674
"That is a very dangerous snake!"

10
00:00:40.754 --> 00:00:44.075
For three days we—and our animals—stayed inside.

11
00:00:44.835 --> 00:00:47.116
The ducks and geese were still out on the marsh.

12
00:00:47.956 --> 00:00:51.537
I knew the snake must be lurking in the reeds because the birds were so restless.

13
00:00:52.357 --> 00:00:54.237
We were running out of hay for the animals.

14
00:00:55.078 --> 00:00:58.559
I was debating what to do when I looked out and saw the snake approaching again.

15
00:00:59.419 --> 00:01:03.160
Grabbing my gun, I fired at the snake, and this time I did not miss.

16
00:01:04.252 --> 00:01:08.274
After this episode I decided to take my family to our farm at Woodlands.

17
00:01:13.576 --> 00:01:18.078
The boys were soon bored, so I said they could go on an expedition without me.

18
00:01:18.878 --> 00:01:20.799
I wanted to encourage their independence.

19
00:01:21.819 --> 00:01:24.440
"I'd rather stay here with you and Mother," said Ernest.

20
00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:27.242
"Not me," said little Franz.

21
00:01:27.262 --> 00:01:29.843
"I can't wait to go exploring!"

22
00:01:30.643 --> 00:01:32.464
I would have preferred to keep Franz with us.

23
00:01:33.464 --> 00:01:36.745
But I had made the offer to all the boys, so I couldn't withdraw it.

24
00:01:37.466 --> 00:01:39.547
In high spirits, the three boys rode off.

25
00:01:40.427 --> 00:01:42.388
All day I wondered about their adventures.

26
00:01:44.469 --> 00:01:47.370
At sunset they returned and joined us around the campfire.

27
00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:49.811
"We had a splendid ride!"

28
00:01:50.131 --> 00:01:50.691
said Jack.

29
00:01:50.711 --> 00:01:53.552
"We went all the way through the Gap!"

30
00:01:53.932 --> 00:01:54.773
said little Franz.

31
00:01:55.693 --> 00:01:59.575
He was talking about a narrow pass we'd previously found through the rocky hills.

32
00:01:59.595 --> 00:02:03.729
"They're forgetting the most important thing," Fritz said impatiently.

33
00:02:03.749 --> 00:02:06.932
"We drove a herd of antelope right through the Gap!

34
00:02:07.672 --> 00:02:09.454
The antelope are in our territory now.

35
00:02:10.274 --> 00:02:11.936
All ready for us to hunt or tame."

36
00:02:11.956 --> 00:02:13.997
"That's wonderful," I said.

37
00:02:14.017 --> 00:02:15.919
"But what's to keep them there, Fritz?"

38
00:02:15.939 --> 00:02:22.364
"We strung a line across the pass—" "And hung feathers and rags from it," interrupted Franz.

39
00:02:22.384 --> 00:02:26.888
"Those things will dance in the wind and spook the antelope," explained Fritz.

40
00:02:26.908 --> 00:02:29.770
"I'm sure the herd won't try to go through the Gap."

41
00:02:30.651 --> 00:02:31.772
"Well done," I said.

42
00:02:32.128 --> 00:02:34.350
"Tomorrow I'd like to go out riding with you."

43
00:02:37.672 --> 00:02:38.993
The next day we rode to the Gap.

44
00:02:39.774 --> 00:02:41.755
I helped the boys to build a fence of bamboo.

45
00:02:41.775 --> 00:02:45.658
"That ought to keep the antelope on our side of the Gap," I said.

46
00:02:46.519 --> 00:02:50.702
We were on our way back to Woodlands when Jack and Franz decided to gallop ahead.

47
00:02:51.723 --> 00:02:54.645
Suddenly four ostriches rose up from where they'd been resting.

48
00:02:55.405 --> 00:02:58.628
Jack brought down the large male ostrich with his lasso.

49
00:02:59.348 --> 00:03:02.450
We leaped from our steeds and tried to approach the captured bird.

50
00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:06.552
He kicked and struggled so hard that I feared we'd have to let him go.

51
00:03:06.572 --> 00:03:09.874
"If only we could block out the light," said Fritz.

52
00:03:09.894 --> 00:03:14.236
"Sometimes that calms birds—like putting a cover over a parrot's cage."

53
00:03:14.997 --> 00:03:17.939
I threw my coat over the bird's head, and he stopped struggling.

54
00:03:18.659 --> 00:03:20.180
"How are we going to get him home?"

55
00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:21.140
asked Jack.

56
00:03:21.941 --> 00:03:27.064
"I've heard it's possible to tame a wild elephant by making it walk between two tame elephants," I

57
00:03:27.124 --> 00:03:29.248
said. "Maybe that would work here."

58
00:03:29.268 --> 00:03:34.730
"But, Father, we don't have any tame ostriches," said little Franz.

59
00:03:35.630 --> 00:03:37.891
"We have Storm and Grumble," Jack pointed out.

60
00:03:37.911 --> 00:03:40.272
"I don't think they'll object to helping us."

61
00:03:41.212 --> 00:03:44.153
So we fastened the ostrich between the buffalo and the bull.

62
00:03:44.893 --> 00:03:46.613
Franz and Jack mounted their steeds.

63
00:03:47.514 --> 00:03:49.554
I removed my coat from the ostrich's head.

64
00:03:50.534 --> 00:03:52.815
For a few moments he lay perfectly still.

65
00:03:53.655 --> 00:03:55.536
Then he bounded up and tried to escape.

66
00:03:56.332 --> 00:03:58.894
But Storm and Grumble weren't about to let him do that.

67
00:03:59.714 --> 00:04:01.275
We slowly walked our prisoner home.

68
00:04:02.256 --> 00:04:06.118
As we approached, I could see the look of dismay on my wife's face.

69
00:04:06.138 --> 00:04:08.079
"See what we've got!"

70
00:04:08.319 --> 00:04:09.240
crowed little Franz.

71
00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:11.661
"An ostrich!"

72
00:04:11.681 --> 00:04:14.122
"Another mouth to feed!"

73
00:04:14.403 --> 00:04:15.323
my wife exclaimed.

74
00:04:15.343 --> 00:04:18.005
"You've brought home a useless pet!"

75
00:04:18.025 --> 00:04:21.487
"He's not useless, Mother," protested Jack.

76
00:04:22.027 --> 00:04:23.508
"You should see how fast he runs.

77
00:04:23.904 --> 00:04:26.226
He'll be the fastest animal in our stables.

78
00:04:27.086 --> 00:04:28.007
Just wait and see."

79
00:04:29.748 --> 00:04:31.930
A month later the ostrich was completely tame.

80
00:04:32.810 --> 00:04:34.051
Jack named him Hurricane.

81
00:04:34.832 --> 00:04:36.653
I made a saddle and a special bridle.

82
00:04:37.574 --> 00:04:40.196
The reins were attached to flaps over the bird's eyes.

83
00:04:41.136 --> 00:04:44.098
With both flaps open, the bird galloped straight ahead.

84
00:04:44.999 --> 00:04:46.940
Cover his right eye and he turned left.

85
00:04:47.661 --> 00:04:49.482
Cover his left eye and he turned right.

86
00:04:50.243 --> 00:04:51.804
Cover both eyes and he stopped.

87
00:04:52.813 --> 00:04:55.879
Jack took him out for a ride, and the bird galloped away.
